const {H1,H2,H3,P,STORY,CASE,TIP,DIG,SEC,LN,PB,TBL}=require("./lib");
const c=[];

// ---------- EP 01 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 01 — The Handmaid of Justice: Sangram Singh and the Nature of Civil Procedure"));
c.push(H2("Covers: What civil procedure is for · Inquisitorial and adversary systems · History from 1712 to 1908 · Structure and principal features of the Code · Sections versus Orders and the power of High Courts and States to amend · Jurisdiction of civil courts (Sec 9) · Key definitions (Sec 2)"));
c.push(STORY("Kotah, Rajasthan, 1952. Sangram Singh has just lost an election petition. When the Election Tribunal took up the case his lawyer did not appear on the appointed day, the Tribunal proceeded ex parte, examined the petitioner's witnesses, and on the next date refused to let Sangram Singh's counsel cross-examine them or lead evidence, because he had 'been proceeded against ex parte'. The Tribunal set aside his election. He went to the Supreme Court, and in 1955 Justice Vivian Bose wrote the sentence that every civil lawyer in India quotes: a code of procedure is 'designed to facilitate justice and further its ends: not a penal enactment for punishment and penalties; not a thing designed to trip people up'. Order IX, the Court explained, does not shut a defendant out of the trial for a single default; it lets the court proceed in his absence on that day, and he may join at the next hearing, though he cannot be allowed to reopen what has been done. The Tribunal's order was set aside. That is the whole spirit of the Code of Civil Procedure: rules exist so that disputes are decided on their merits, in an orderly way, with each side heard — and the rules must not be turned into traps. This episode is about the history that produced those rules, the two ways a legal system can find facts, and the first gate of every civil case: does this court have jurisdiction at all?"));
c.push(H3("Two models of procedure"));
c.push(P("In the **inquisitorial** model (France, Germany, and Indian criminal inquiries) the judge takes charge of collecting evidence and questioning witnesses. In the **adversary** model (England, India) the parties define the dispute through pleadings, gather and present their own evidence, and the judge is a neutral umpire who decides on what is placed before him — the judge 'holds the balance' (Sangram Singh). The Code is adversarial but with inquisitorial powers where fairness requires them: the court may frame issues from the material on record (Order XIV), examine parties itself (Order X), appoint commissioners to investigate (Order XXVI), call witnesses of its own motion (Order XVI Rule 14), and use its inherent power (Sec 151). Before the British, Hindu and Muslim law knew royal courts, village panchayats and caste tribunals with informal procedure; the Mayor's Courts under the Charter of 1726 in Madras, Bombay and Calcutta introduced English procedure; the Regulations of the Companies' Governments (1772 onwards, Warren Hastings' Diwani Adalats) governed the mofussil; the Charter of 1862 created the High Courts. The first uniform Code of Civil Procedure was Act VIII of **1859**; it was replaced in **1877**, revised in **1882**, and then re-enacted as the present Code, **Act V of 1908**, which came into force on 1 January 1909. Major amendments: 1951 and 1956 (extension to the whole of India), **1976** (Act 104 — Sec 25 transfer by the Supreme Court, Order X, Sec 100 substantial question, the Explanation to Sec 11 on public interest suits, abolition of second appeals in small cases), **1999 and 2002** (Acts 46 of 1999 and 22 of 2002, in force from 1 July 2002 — Sec 89 settlement outside court, time limits for written statements and adjournments, Order XVIII Rule 4 evidence on affidavit, Sec 100A no letters patent appeal, Sec 102 no second appeal for claims up to twenty-five thousand rupees, Sec 115 revision curtailed, Order VI Rule 17 proviso), the Commercial Courts Act **2015** amendments for commercial disputes (Order XIII-A summary judgment, Order XV-A case management, actual costs), and the 2018 amendment of the Commercial Courts Act with pre-institution mediation."));
c.push(H3("Structure and principal features"));
c.push(P("The Code has **158 sections in the body**, which lay down the general principles and can be amended only by Parliament (with State amendments under Art 254 of the Constitution), and a **First Schedule of 51 Orders** containing the rules of detail, which **High Courts may amend under Sec 122** (with the previous approval of the State Government, Sec 126) and State Governments may adapt, so long as the rules are consistent with the body of the Code (Sec 128). This is why State amendments matter: Andhra Pradesh, Maharashtra, Punjab and others have their own versions of Order V, Order XXI and Order XXXIX, and a lawyer must read the local rules. Other features: the Code is territorial (Sec 1 — extends to the whole of India except Nagaland and tribal areas); it applies to all civil courts unless a special law excludes it (Sec 4 saving of special laws; Sec 5 revenue courts); it is largely procedural and therefore retrospective in operation; it consolidates rather than exhaustively codifies — the court retains inherent powers (Sec 151) to do justice where the Code is silent; it combines general provisions (Part I suits in general, Sec 9-35B) with special suits (Part IV, Sec 79-93), incidental proceedings (Sec 75-78), supplemental proceedings (Sec 94-95), appeals, reference, review and revision (Sec 96-115), and miscellaneous provisions. Observance of procedure is important because it secures equality between litigants, predictability, and finality — but the Code's own Sec 99 and Sec 21 say that a decree is not to be reversed for a mere irregularity that has caused no failure of justice: 'a procedural rule is the handmaid of justice, not its mistress' (Sangram Singh; Ghanshyam Dass v. Dominion of India, 1984; State of Punjab v. Shamlal Murari, 1976 — 'processual law is not to be a tyrant but a servant')."));
c.push(H3("Definitions to memorise — Sec 2"));
c.push(SEC("Decree 2(2)","The formal expression of an adjudication which, so far as the court expressing it is concerned, conclusively determines the rights of the parties with regard to all or any of the matters in controversy in the suit; may be preliminary (further proceedings needed, e.g. partition, mortgage, accounts) or final, or partly both; includes the rejection of a plaint (Order VII Rule 11) and the determination of a question under Sec 144 (restitution); excludes an order of dismissal for default and an appealable order. Essentials: adjudication, in a suit, determining rights of parties, conclusive, formal expression. Shankar Balwant Lokhande v. Chandrakant (1995) — a preliminary decree in a partition suit that leaves the shares to be worked out is a decree."));
c.push(SEC("Order 2(14)","The formal expression of any decision of a civil court which is not a decree — e.g. an order on an application for injunction, for amendment, for adjournment. Orders are appealable only if listed in Sec 104 and Order XLIII."));
c.push(SEC("Judgment 2(9)","The statement given by the judge of the grounds of a decree or order; Order XX Rule 4 — a concise statement of the case, the points for determination, the decision and the reasons (Balraj Taneja v. Sunil Madan, 1999 — a judgment must be reasoned even when the defendant is absent)."));
c.push(SEC("Decree-holder 2(3) / Judgment-debtor 2(10)","The person in whose favour a decree or an order capable of execution is passed; the person against whom it is passed."));
c.push(SEC("Legal representative 2(11)","A person who in law represents the estate of a deceased person, including an intermeddler and the person on whom the estate devolves — wider than 'heir' (Custodian of Branches of BANCO National Ultramarino v. Nalini Bai Naique, 1989)."));
c.push(SEC("Mesne profits 2(12)","Profits which the person in wrongful possession actually received or might with ordinary diligence have received, together with interest, but not profits due to improvements made by him (Lucy Kochuvareed v. P. Mariappa Gounder, 1979)."));
c.push(SEC("Foreign court 2(5) / Foreign judgment 2(6)","A court situated outside India and not established by the Central Government; the judgment of such a court — relevant to Sec 13-14 and Sec 44A."));
c.push(SEC("Suit","Not defined; a civil proceeding instituted by the presentation of a plaint (Sec 26, Order IV); Hansraj Gupta v. Official Liquidator (1933)."));
c.push(H3("Jurisdiction — Sec 9 and the bars"));
c.push(P("**Sec 9:** the courts shall have jurisdiction to try all suits of a civil nature excepting suits of which their cognizance is either expressly or impliedly barred; Explanation I — a suit in which the right to property or to an office is contested is a suit of a civil nature notwithstanding that the right depends entirely on a decision of questions as to religious rites or ceremonies; Explanation II — it is immaterial whether any fees are attached to the office. The presumption is in favour of jurisdiction; exclusion must be express or clearly implied (Dhulabhai v. State of M.P., 1969 — seven propositions on when a statute creating a special tribunal excludes the civil court: exclusion is not readily inferred, and even where a statute gives finality, the civil court can examine whether the provisions of the Act were complied with and whether the tribunal acted in conformity with the fundamental principles of judicial procedure; Secretary of State v. Mask & Co., 1940; Premier Automobiles v. Kamlekar Shantaram Wadke, 1975 — industrial disputes; Abdul Waheed Khan v. Bhawani, 1966; Rajasthan SRTC v. Bal Mukund Bairwa, 2009). Kinds of jurisdiction: **subject-matter** (small cause courts, family courts, rent tribunals — a decree without it is a nullity, Kiran Singh v. Chaman Paswan, 1954), **pecuniary** (Sec 6 and 15 — the suit goes to the court of the lowest grade competent to try it; objections must be taken at the earliest, Sec 21), **territorial** (Sec 16-20), and **original and appellate**. Consent cannot confer jurisdiction where none exists, but a party who submits to a court's territorial or pecuniary jurisdiction cannot later object unless there was a failure of justice (Sec 21; Hira Lal Patni v. Kali Nath, 1962; Harshad Chiman Lal Modi v. DLF Universal, 2005 — objection to subject-matter jurisdiction can be raised at any stage). A **civil right** is a private right of person or property, as opposed to a purely religious, political or social question: suits about caste questions, religious rites alone, or voluntary payments are not civil; suits about the right to worship, an office in a temple, expulsion from a club affecting property, and dignities with fees are civil (Sinha Ramanuja Jeer v. Ranga Ramanuja Jeer, 1961; Ugamsingh v. Kesrimal, 1971; Most Rev. P.M.A. Metropolitan v. Moran Mar Marthoma, 1995 — the right to worship and the office of Malankara Metropolitan are civil rights). Sec 9A (Maharashtra amendment) makes jurisdiction a preliminary issue."));
c.push(CASE("Sangram Singh v. Election Tribunal, Kotah AIR 1955 SC 425","The Code is meant to facilitate justice and is not a penal enactment designed to trip people up; a defendant absent on one hearing may participate at later hearings though he cannot reopen what was done; procedure is the handmaid of justice."));
c.push(CASE("Dhulabhai v. State of M.P. AIR 1969 SC 78","Seven propositions on ouster of civil court jurisdiction by special statutes; where the statute gives finality and adequate remedies the civil court is excluded, but it can always examine whether the statutory procedure was followed and whether the provision is ultra vires."));
c.push(CASE("Kiran Singh v. Chaman Paswan AIR 1954 SC 340","A decree passed by a court without inherent (subject-matter) jurisdiction is a nullity that can be challenged even in execution; objections to pecuniary and territorial jurisdiction are governed by Sec 21 and require prejudice."));
c.push(CASE("Most Rev. P.M.A. Metropolitan v. Moran Mar Marthoma (1995)","The scope of Sec 9 is wide; a suit relating to religious offices carrying rights is a suit of a civil nature; the civil court's jurisdiction is the rule and its exclusion the exception."));
c.push(DIG("The e-Courts project has made the Code's territorial rules practical for online filing: e-filing portals route the plaint to the court of competent territorial and pecuniary jurisdiction, and the National Judicial Data Grid tracks every stage from Sec 26 institution to Order XXI execution. Suits about online defamation, e-commerce contracts and domain names raise Sec 9 and Sec 20 questions daily — a civil court has jurisdiction over a contract concluded on a website (Sec 9) and the place where the buyer received the goods or the seller carries on business gives Sec 20 jurisdiction, subject to a valid exclusive-jurisdiction clause. The 2015 Commercial Courts Act moves high-value commercial suits, including IT and e-commerce disputes, to Commercial Courts with the same Code and additional Orders."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Decree', 'Order', 'Judgment', 'Mesne profits', 'Legal representative', 'Preliminary and final decree', 'Suit of a civil nature' — three marks each: definition, section, one example or case. Part B: 'Every suit of a civil nature is triable by a civil court unless barred — discuss Sec 9 with the exceptions' — text of Sec 9, Explanations, civil nature test, express and implied bars, Dhulabhai propositions, examples; or 'Trace the evolution and salient features of the Code'. Part C: a suit about the right to perform puja in a temple with fees attached — civil (Explanation I and II); a suit against an assessment under a taxing statute with a complete machinery — barred unless the Dhulabhai exceptions apply."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 02 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 02 — The Carrier Who Chose the Court: Patel Roadways and the Anatomy of a Suit"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Parties to a suit (Order I) · Frame of suit and splitting of claims (Order II) · Institution (Sec 26, Order IV) · Place of suing (Sec 15-20) · Cause of action and jurisdictional objections (Sec 21) · Summons (Sec 27-29, 31, Order V) · Discovery and inspection (Sec 30, Order XI)"));
c.push(STORY("Bombay, 1991. Patel Roadways, a transport company with its head office in Bombay and branch offices across the country, is sued by Prasad Trading Company in Madras for goods that were damaged in transit from a branch at Madras. Patel Roadways says the suit can only be brought in Bombay, where its principal office is, because the consignment note said so and because a corporation 'carries on business' where its head office is. The Supreme Court read the Explanation to Section 20 and gave the answer that governs every suit against a company: a corporation carries on business at its sole or principal office, or, in respect of a cause of action arising at a place where it has a subordinate office, at that place — the Explanation is meant to widen the plaintiff's choice, not to allow a company with a branch everywhere to be sued anywhere. Madras had jurisdiction because the branch was there and the cause of action arose there; an agreement choosing one of two competent courts is valid (Hakam Singh v. Gammon India, 1971), but it cannot confer jurisdiction on a court that has none. From this small dispute about damaged goods the whole first chapter of a lawsuit unfolds — who must be parties, what must be put in one suit, where it is filed, how the defendant is summoned, and how each side forces the other to disclose its documents."));
c.push(H3("Parties — Order I"));
c.push(P("Rule 1: all persons may be joined as plaintiffs where the right to relief arises out of the same act or transaction and a common question of law or fact would arise; Rule 3: the same for defendants (joinder). Rule 2 and 3A: the court may order separate trials to avoid embarrassment. Rule 8: **representative suits** — one or more persons with the same interest may sue or be sued on behalf of all with the court's permission, with notice to all interested by public advertisement; a decree binds all represented (Explanation to Sec 11 — Explanation VI) (T.N. Housing Board v. T.N. Ganapathy, 1990 — same interest, not identical cause of action). Rule 9: no suit is defeated by misjoinder or non-joinder of parties **except non-joinder of a necessary party**. A **necessary party** is one without whom no effective decree can be passed; a **proper party** is one whose presence is needed for a complete and final decision but in whose absence a decree can still be passed (Kasturi v. Iyyamperumal, 2005 — in a suit for specific performance, a stranger claiming title is not a necessary party; Udit Narain Singh Malpaharia v. Board of Revenue, 1963). Rule 10: striking out, adding or substituting parties at any stage — a person may be added as plaintiff only with his consent; the suit is deemed instituted against an added defendant on the date of addition for limitation (Rule 10(5)). Rule 13: objections as to non-joinder must be taken at the earliest opportunity. Rule 11: conduct of suit. Rule 12: appearance of one of several plaintiffs or defendants for others."));
c.push(H3("Frame of suit — Order II"));
c.push(P("Rule 1: every suit shall be framed to afford ground for final decision on the subjects in dispute and to prevent further litigation. **Rule 2 — the rule against splitting of claims:** every suit shall include the whole of the claim which the plaintiff is entitled to make in respect of the cause of action; if he omits or intentionally relinquishes any portion he cannot afterwards sue for it (Rule 2(2)); and a person entitled to more than one relief in respect of the same cause of action who omits to sue for all without the leave of the court cannot afterwards sue for the omitted relief (Rule 2(3)). The test is identity of the cause of action — the bundle of facts the plaintiff must prove — not the identity of the relief (Gurbux Singh v. Bhooralal, 1964 — the defendant must prove the earlier plaint to raise the bar; Deva Ram v. Ishwar Chand, 1995; Virgo Industries v. Venturetech Solutions, 2013 — a suit for injunction and a later suit for specific performance on the same agreement: leave required). Rule 3: joinder of causes of action against the same defendants; Rule 4: only certain claims may be joined with a suit for recovery of immovable property (mesne profits, damages for breach of the same contract, claims where the cause of action is the same); Rule 6: the court may order separate trials to avoid delay or embarrassment; Rule 7: objections on misjoinder must be taken at the earliest."));
c.push(H3("Institution and the place of suing"));
c.push(P("**Sec 26 and Order IV:** every suit is instituted by presenting a plaint in duplicate; facts must be proved by affidavit (2002; Order VI Rule 15(4)); the suit is registered in the register of civil suits. **Sec 15:** every suit is instituted in the court of the lowest grade competent to try it — a rule of procedure for the convenience of courts, so a decree by a higher court is not a nullity. **Sec 16:** suits for recovery, partition, foreclosure, sale or redemption, determination of any other right to or interest in immovable property, compensation for wrong to immovable property, and recovery of movable property under distraint or attachment, are instituted where the property is situate — with a proviso that a suit for relief respecting immovable property held by the defendant may be brought where the defendant resides or carries on business if the relief can be obtained through his personal obedience (the equity rule). **Sec 17:** property in the jurisdiction of different courts — any of them. **Sec 18:** where the local limits are uncertain. **Sec 19:** compensation for wrong to person or movables — where the wrong was done or where the defendant resides. **Sec 20:** all other suits — where the defendant (or any of them, with leave or acquiescence of the others) actually and voluntarily resides, carries on business or personally works for gain, or where the cause of action wholly or in part arises; Explanation: a corporation carries on business at its sole or principal office, or at a subordinate office in respect of a cause of action arising there (Patel Roadways; New Moga Transport v. United India Insurance, 2004). **Cause of action** means every fact which it is necessary for the plaintiff to prove to obtain a judgment — the bundle of facts — not every piece of evidence (A.B.C. Laminart v. A.P. Agencies, 1989 — where two courts have jurisdiction the parties may choose one by contract; the words 'alone', 'only', 'exclusive' are not necessary if the intention is clear; Swastik Gases v. Indian Oil, 2013). **Sec 21:** objections to territorial or pecuniary jurisdiction, and to the competence of the executing court, must be taken in the court of first instance at the earliest possible opportunity, before issues are settled, and will be allowed by an appellate or revisional court only if there has been a consequent failure of justice. **Sec 21A** (1976): no suit lies to set aside a decree on the ground of a place-of-suing objection. **Sec 22-25** transfer: a suit that could be brought in more than one court may be transferred on the defendant's application (Sec 22); the High Court or District Court may transfer or withdraw cases (Sec 24); the Supreme Court may transfer a case from one State to another for the ends of justice (Sec 25, 1976 — used in matrimonial cases for the wife's convenience, Sumita Singh v. Kumar Sanjay, 2001)."));
c.push(H3("Summons — Sec 27-29, 31 and Order V"));
c.push(P("When a suit is instituted, a summons may issue to the defendant to appear and answer, served in the prescribed manner, on a day not beyond thirty days from institution (Sec 27, 1999). **Order V:** Rule 1 — summons to appear, either to settle issues or for final disposal, with a copy of the plaint; the defendant may appear in person or by pleader; no summons is needed if the defendant appears at presentation and admits the claim; Rule 2 — copy of plaint annexed; Rule 9 (2002) — delivery by the court through its officer or by registered post, speed post, courier approved by the High Court, fax, e-mail or other means; refusal to take delivery is deemed service (Rule 9(5)); Rule 9A — the plaintiff may take the summons for direct service; Rule 10-16 — personal service on the defendant, or on an agent, or on an adult family member (not a servant); Rule 17 — **affixing** on the outer door when the defendant cannot be found and has no agent, with a report; Rule 19 — examination of the serving officer; Rule 20 — **substituted service** where the defendant is keeping out of the way: affixing at the court-house and the house, and (2002) publication in a daily newspaper circulating in the locality, which is effective service; Rule 21-30 — service on defendants in other jurisdictions, in prison, of public officers and soldiers (Sec 28-29). **Sec 29:** foreign summons — a summons issued by a court outside India of certain categories (the Central Government's notified courts, courts in reciprocating territories) may be sent to Indian courts and served as if issued here. **Sec 31:** summons to witnesses — the provisions on summons to defendants apply to witnesses (Order XVI). Sec 30 gives the court power, on application or of its own motion, to make orders on discovery, inspection, interrogatories, admission of documents and facts, issue summons for documents, and order facts to be proved by affidavit — the statutory anchor for **Order XI**: Rule 1-11 interrogatories (questions in writing to the opposite party, answered on affidavit, with leave; not for evidence but for facts); Rule 12-14 **discovery of documents** — an order to disclose on affidavit all documents in the party's possession relating to the matter, privileged documents being described but not produced; Rule 15-19 inspection; Rule 21 non-compliance may lead to dismissal of the suit or striking out of the defence (Babbar Sewing Machine Co. v. Trilok Nath Mahajan, 1978 — only in extreme cases). For commercial suits Order XI has been rewritten by the 2015 Act to require disclosure of all documents with the plaint."));
c.push(CASE("Patel Roadways Ltd. v. Prasad Trading Co. (1991) 4 SCC 270","The Explanation to Sec 20 confines a corporation's 'carrying on business' to the principal office, or the subordinate office where the cause of action arose; a plaintiff cannot be forced to the head office when the branch dealt with him."));
c.push(CASE("A.B.C. Laminart v. A.P. Agencies AIR 1989 SC 1239","Cause of action is the bundle of facts to be proved; where more than one court has jurisdiction the parties may agree to one of them; a clause excluding other courts is valid and need not use the word 'only'."));
c.push(CASE("Gurbux Singh v. Bhooralal AIR 1964 SC 1810","Order II Rule 2 bars a later suit only if the cause of action in both suits is identical; the defendant must place the previous plaint on record to prove the bar."));
c.push(CASE("Kasturi v. Iyyamperumal (2005) 6 SCC 733","Two tests for a necessary party: a right to relief against him in respect of the controversy, and no effective decree in his absence; a stranger claiming independent title is not a necessary party to a specific performance suit."));
c.push(DIG("Order V Rule 9 already authorises service by e-mail and fax; courts now serve summons by WhatsApp and other messaging services under the Supreme Court's 2020 directions (In re: Cognizance for Extension of Limitation) and the e-Committee's rules, with the blue-tick or delivery report as the serving officer's report. Sec 20 questions in internet transactions are decided by where the server, the buyer and the seller are: the place where a website is accessed does not by itself give jurisdiction unless the defendant purposefully directed business there (Banyan Tree Holding v. A. Murali Krishna Reddy, Delhi HC 2009). Order XI discovery in commercial suits now means electronic discovery — e-mails, chats and database entries — which the Commercial Courts Act requires to be disclosed with the plaint."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Necessary and proper party', 'Representative suit', 'Order II Rule 2', 'Cause of action', 'Substituted service', 'Interrogatories'. Part B: 'Discuss the provisions relating to the place of suing under Sec 15 to 20' — the ladder of Sec 15, 16 and its proviso, 17-19, 20 with its three limbs and the Explanation, Sec 21 objections, and A.B.C. Laminart on exclusive-jurisdiction clauses; or 'Explain the rule against splitting of claims and its exceptions'. Part C: a company with head office in Hyderabad and a branch in Vijayawada contracts through the branch — suit lies in Vijayawada (Patel Roadways); a plaintiff sues for rent and later for eviction on the same lease — Order II Rule 2 applies only if the cause of action is the same."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 03 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 03 — The Same Fight Twice: Daryao and the Bars of Res Sub Judice and Res Judicata"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Stay of suit — res sub judice (Sec 10) · Res judicata (Sec 11) and its eight Explanations · Constructive res judicata · Res judicata in writs and public interest litigation · Bar on further suit (Sec 12) · Foreign judgments (Sec 13-14, 44A)"));
c.push(STORY("Uttar Pradesh, 1961. Daryao and other tenants were evicted from their land during the disturbances of 1947; when they returned, the landlords would not let them back. They filed a writ petition under Article 226 in the Allahabad High Court, which was dismissed on merits. They then filed a fresh petition in the Supreme Court under Article 32, arguing that a fundamental right can never be shut out by a technical plea of res judicata. Chief Justice Gajendragadkar's Constitution Bench disagreed: the rule of res judicata is not a technicality but a principle of public policy founded on the need for finality; a decision of a High Court on merits in a writ petition binds the parties and bars a petition under Article 32 on the same facts — though a dismissal in limine without reasons, or on the ground of delay or an alternative remedy, does not. Daryao lost, and the Supreme Court gained a rule it uses every week. Sections 10 and 11 are the Code's answer to two questions that arise whenever a litigant tries his luck a second time: should the second suit wait for the first (res sub judice), and has the matter already been decided (res judicata)? Behind both lie three Latin maxims: interest reipublicae ut sit finis litium — it is in the interest of the State that there be an end to litigation; nemo debet bis vexari pro una et eadem causa — no one should be vexed twice for the same cause; and res judicata pro veritate accipitur — a judicial decision is accepted as correct."));
c.push(H3("Res sub judice — Sec 10"));
c.push(P("No court shall proceed with the trial of any suit in which the matter in issue is also **directly and substantially** in issue in a **previously instituted suit** between the same parties, or parties under whom they claim, litigating under the same title, where that suit is pending in the same or any other court in India having jurisdiction to grant the relief claimed, or in a court beyond India established by the Central Government, or before the Supreme Court. Explanation: the pendency of a suit in a foreign court does not preclude the Indian court. Conditions: two suits; the matter in issue in the later suit directly and substantially in issue in the earlier; same parties or their privies; same title; the earlier court competent to grant the relief in the later suit; the earlier suit pending. The section bars the **trial**, not the institution — the later suit is stayed, and the court may still pass interlocutory orders such as injunctions or attachment (Indian Bank v. Maharashtra State Co-operative Marketing Federation, 1998 — 'trial' means the final hearing, so a summary judgment application under Order XXXVII is not barred). The test is whether the decision in the earlier suit would operate as res judicata in the later one (Manohar Lal Chopra v. Seth Hiralal, 1962 — the court also has inherent power under Sec 151 to stay a suit where Sec 10 does not strictly apply). A decree passed in violation of Sec 10 is not a nullity; the parties may waive the objection (Pukhraj D. Jain v. G. Gopalakrishna, 2004)."));
c.push(H3("Res judicata — Sec 11"));
c.push(P("No court shall try any suit or issue in which the matter **directly and substantially in issue** has been directly and substantially in issue in a **former suit** between the same parties, or between parties under whom they or any of them claim, litigating under the same title, in a court **competent** to try such subsequent suit or the suit in which such issue has been subsequently raised, and has been **heard and finally decided** by such court. Six conditions: (1) the matter directly and substantially in issue — not collaterally or incidentally (Explanation III: the matter must have been alleged by one party and denied or admitted by the other; Sajjadanashin Sayed v. Musa Dadabhai Ummer, 2000 — a matter is directly in issue if the decision was necessary for the earlier judgment); (2) same parties or their privies (Explanation VI — persons litigating bona fide in respect of a public right or a private right claimed in common are deemed to claim under each other — the representative-suit and PIL extension; Forward Construction Co. v. Prabhat Mandal, 1986 — a PIL decision binds later PILs on the same issue); (3) same title — the capacity in which the party litigated; (4) a court competent to try the subsequent suit — since 1976, Explanation VIII: a decision of a court of limited jurisdiction, competent to decide the issue, operates as res judicata even though that court was not competent to try the subsequent suit (Sulochana Amma v. Narayanan Nair, 1994 — overruling the earlier view); pecuniary competence is no longer a bar; (5) heard and finally decided — on merits, not dismissed for default or for want of jurisdiction, and not an ex parte decree that was later set aside (Explanation V: a relief claimed and not expressly granted is deemed refused); (6) 'former suit' means a suit decided earlier, even if instituted later (Explanation I). Explanation IV — **constructive res judicata**: any matter which might and ought to have been made a ground of defence or attack in the former suit is deemed to have been directly and substantially in issue — a party cannot keep back a ground and use it in a second round (State of U.P. v. Nawab Hussain, 1977 — a dismissed constable who challenged his dismissal on one ground in a writ could not raise a different ground in a later suit; Devilal Modi v. Sales Tax Officer, 1965 — constructive res judicata applies to writ petitions; Direct Recruit Class II Engineering Officers' Association v. State of Maharashtra, 1990 — a Constitution Bench applied it to service matters). Explanation VII (1976): the section applies to execution proceedings — a question decided at one stage of execution binds later stages. Explanation II: competence is determined irrespective of any right of appeal. Res judicata applies between co-defendants where there is a conflict of interest between them necessary to be decided for the relief to the plaintiff (Iftikhar Ahmed v. Syed Meharban Ali, 1974; Mahboob Sahab v. Syed Ismail, 1995), and between co-plaintiffs on the same test. It applies to different stages of the same proceeding (Satyadhyan Ghosal v. Deorajin Debi, 1960 — an interlocutory order that decides a matter binds the later stages, but an order that does not terminate the proceeding may be challenged in the appeal from the final decree; Arjun Singh v. Mohindra Kumar, 1964). It does not apply where the earlier decision was without jurisdiction, obtained by fraud (S.P. Chengalvaraya Naidu v. Jagannath, 1994 — fraud unravels everything), on a pure question of law that has since changed (Mathura Prasad Bajoo Jaiswal v. Dossibai N.B. Jeejeebhoy, 1971), or in a proceeding where the court could not go into the question (a dismissal of a writ in limine — Daryao; Workmen of Cochin Port Trust v. Board of Trustees, 1978). Res judicata is a plea of the defendant which must be raised; the court may however take notice of it suo motu if the earlier decision is on record. **Difference from estoppel:** res judicata results from the decision of the court and bars the jurisdiction of the court; estoppel results from the act of the party and bars the party from proving a fact; res judicata is founded on public policy, estoppel on equity; res judicata binds both parties, estoppel binds only the person who made the representation."));
c.push(H3("Sec 12 and foreign judgments — Sec 13-14, 44A"));
c.push(P("**Sec 12:** where a plaintiff is precluded by rules from instituting a further suit in respect of a particular cause of action, he shall not be entitled to institute a suit in respect of it in any court — a general bar that gives effect to Order II Rule 2, Order IX Rule 9 (a plaintiff whose suit is dismissed for default cannot bring a fresh suit on the same cause of action) and Order XXIII Rule 1 (withdrawal without liberty). **Sec 13:** a foreign judgment is **conclusive** as to any matter directly adjudicated upon between the same parties or privies, **except** where (a) it was pronounced by a court of no competent jurisdiction; (b) it was not given on the merits; (c) it is founded on an incorrect view of international law or a refusal to recognise Indian law where applicable; (d) the proceedings were opposed to natural justice; (e) it was obtained by fraud; (f) it sustains a claim founded on a breach of Indian law. **Sec 14:** presumption of competence from a certified copy. **Sec 44A:** a decree of a superior court in a **reciprocating territory** (United Kingdom, Singapore, UAE since 2020, and others notified) may be executed in India as if passed by the District Court, subject to the Sec 13 exceptions; judgments of non-reciprocating countries must be sued upon. Cases: R. Viswanathan v. Rukn-ul-Mulk Syed Abdul Wajid (1963) — the competence is judged by Indian private international law; Y. Narasimha Rao v. Y. Venkata Lakshmi (1991) — a foreign divorce decree is recognised only if the foreign court had jurisdiction under the matrimonial law of the parties and decided on merits under that law with the respondent's participation; International Woollen Mills v. Standard Wool (2001) — an ex parte judgment on merits is 'on the merits'; Satya v. Teja Singh (1975) — a Nevada divorce obtained by pretending residence was a fraud on the court."));
c.push(CASE("Daryao v. State of U.P. AIR 1961 SC 1457","Res judicata is a principle of public policy applying to writ petitions; a High Court decision on merits under Art 226 bars an Art 32 petition on the same matter; a dismissal in limine without a speaking order does not."));
c.push(CASE("State of U.P. v. Nawab Hussain (1977) 2 SCC 806","Constructive res judicata (Explanation IV) applies to writ proceedings and subsequent suits; a ground that could have been raised in the earlier writ cannot found a later suit."));
c.push(CASE("Sulochana Amma v. Narayanan Nair (1994) 2 SCC 14","After Explanation VIII, a decision of a court of limited pecuniary jurisdiction on an issue within its competence operates as res judicata in a later suit before a court of higher jurisdiction."));
c.push(CASE("Satyadhyan Ghosal v. Deorajin Debi AIR 1960 SC 941","Res judicata applies between stages of the same litigation; an interlocutory order that does not finally decide the proceeding can be challenged in the appeal against the final decree."));
c.push(CASE("Y. Narasimha Rao v. Y. Venkata Lakshmi (1991) 3 SCC 451","Rules for recognising foreign matrimonial judgments under Sec 13: jurisdiction under the matrimonial law of the parties, decision on merits under that law, and participation of the respondent."));
c.push(DIG("The plea of res judicata now depends on retrieving the earlier record, which the e-Courts case information system and the National Judicial Data Grid make possible across districts and States; the judgment and decree of the former suit are certified copies obtained online and are public documents under Sec 74 BSA. Foreign judgments from reciprocating territories — including Singapore and the UAE — are executed under Sec 44A with certified copies transmitted electronically; the 2020 notification of the UAE as a reciprocating territory changed recovery against defaulters who fled there. Arbitration awards, which are not judgments, follow their own Sec 36 Arbitration Act route, but the res judicata principle applies to arbitral awards too (K.V. George v. Secretary to Government, 1989)."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Res sub judice', 'Res judicata', 'Constructive res judicata', 'Explanation VIII', 'Foreign judgment', 'Res judicata vs estoppel'. Part B: 'Explain the doctrine of res judicata with its essential conditions and the Explanations to Sec 11' — six conditions with a case each, Explanation IV and VI in detail, writ and PIL application (Daryao, Forward Construction), exceptions (fraud, jurisdiction, pure question of law); or 'Distinguish res sub judice from res judicata'. Part C: a tenant sues for injunction and loses; he then sues for declaration of title on facts he could have pleaded — constructive res judicata; a suit stayed under Sec 10 where the earlier court cannot grant the relief — Sec 10 does not apply."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 04 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 04 — The Vexatious Plaint: Arivandandam and the Pleadings"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Pleadings — material facts, forms, condition precedent, presumptions, striking out and amendment (Order VI) · The plaint — particulars, documents, return and rejection (Order VII) · Written statement, set-off and counter-claim (Order VIII) · Framing of issues (Order XIV)"));
c.push(STORY("Madras, 1977. T. Arivandandam has been fighting T.V. Satyapal over a house for years. He has lost a suit, an appeal, a second appeal, a writ petition, and a special leave petition. He then files yet another suit, on a new theory, and asks for an injunction. Justice Krishna Iyer, hearing the matter in the Supreme Court, called it 'a fraud on the court' and gave trial judges a weapon they now use every day: when a plaint is read as a whole and discloses no cause of action, or is 'manifestly vexatious and meritless, in the sense of not disclosing a clear right to sue', the court should nip it in the bud under Order VII Rule 11 instead of issuing summons and letting the case run for years; and where the court sees a party abusing its process it should consider action under the Penal Code for the offence of instituting a vexatious claim. Arivandandam is thus the story of the plaint that should never have been filed — and of the rules of pleading that decide what a plaint and a written statement must contain, what may be struck out, when they may be amended, and how the court boils the dispute down to issues."));
c.push(H3("Fundamental rules of pleading — Order VI"));
c.push(P("Rule 1: 'pleading' means plaint or written statement. Rule 2 (as amended in 1976 and 1999) lays down the four fundamental rules: every pleading shall contain a statement in a concise form of the **material facts** on which the party relies for his claim or defence, **but not the evidence** by which they are to be proved; pleadings are divided into paragraphs numbered consecutively; dates, sums and numbers are expressed in figures as well as words. **Material facts** are facts which the party must prove to succeed — the facta probanda, as opposed to the facta probantia, the evidence (Udhav Singh v. Madhav Rao Scindia, 1977; Virender Nath Gautam v. Satpal Singh, 2007 — in an election petition, material facts must be pleaded or the petition is dismissed). Rule 3: forms in Appendix A. Rule 4: particulars of misrepresentation, fraud, breach of trust, wilful default or undue influence must be stated with dates and items (Bishundeo Narain v. Seogeni Rai, 1951 — general allegations of fraud are insufficient). Rule 6: a **condition precedent** is implied in the pleading and need not be alleged; its non-performance must be distinctly specified by the other party. Rule 7: no pleading may raise a new ground inconsistent with the previous pleading of the same party (except by amendment). Rule 8: a bare denial of a contract is a denial of the fact, not of its legality. Rule 9: the effect of a document is stated briefly without setting it out. Rule 10: malice, knowledge and other conditions of mind may be alleged as facts without particulars. Rule 11: notice may be alleged as a fact without setting out its form. Rule 12: implied contracts may be alleged as facts. Rule 13: **presumptions of law** — a party need not plead a fact which the law presumes in his favour or as to which the burden lies on the other side. Rule 14-15: every pleading is signed by the party and his pleader and **verified** at the foot by the party or a person acquainted with the facts, stating which paragraphs are true to his knowledge and which on information received and believed; since 1999 an affidavit in support is required (Rule 15(4)). Rule 16: the court may **strike out** any matter that is unnecessary, scandalous, frivolous or vexatious, tends to prejudice, embarrass or delay the fair trial, or is an abuse of the process. **Rule 17 — amendment:** the court may at any stage allow either party to alter or amend his pleadings in such manner and on such terms as may be just, and all such amendments shall be made as may be necessary for determining the real questions in controversy — with the 2002 proviso that no amendment shall be allowed after the trial has commenced unless the court concludes that in spite of due diligence the party could not have raised the matter before. Principles (Revajeetu Builders v. Narayanaswamy, 2009 — a summary of the law): amendment is allowed liberally if it is necessary to decide the real controversy, does not cause injustice that costs cannot compensate, does not set up a time-barred claim unless the facts justify it, does not change the nature of the suit or take away a right accrued to the other side, and is not mala fide; delay alone is not a ground for refusal but it may be after the 2002 proviso (Salem Advocate Bar Association (II), 2005); an amendment relates back to the date of the suit unless the court directs otherwise; refusal to allow an amendment may be challenged in revision (now under Art 227) and in the appeal from the decree (Sec 105). Rule 18: failure to amend within the time fixed — the party may not amend after that without leave."));
c.push(H3("The plaint — Order VII"));
c.push(P("Rule 1: the plaint contains the name of the court, the name, description and place of residence of the plaintiff and defendant, a statement that the plaintiff or defendant is a minor or of unsound mind, the **facts constituting the cause of action and when it arose**, the facts showing the court has jurisdiction, the relief claimed, the amount of set-off allowed or relinquished, and a **statement of the value** of the subject matter for jurisdiction and court fees. Rule 2: money suits state the precise amount; mesne profits or an amount to be found on accounts state the approximate amount. Rule 3: immovable property is described sufficiently to identify it — boundaries or survey numbers. Rule 4: a plaintiff suing in a representative character shows his actual interest and the steps taken to qualify. Rule 5: the **defendant's interest and liability** must be shown. Rule 6: where the suit is instituted after the period of limitation, the plaint shall show the **ground upon which exemption from limitation is claimed** (and the court may permit a ground not set out if it is not inconsistent, 1976 proviso). Rule 7: the relief must be stated specifically or in the alternative; general or other relief need not be asked for. Rule 8: where relief is founded on separate grounds they are stated separately. Rule 9: procedure on admitting the plaint — copies for service (1999). **Rule 10 — return of plaint:** at any stage the plaint shall be returned to be presented to the proper court where the suit should have been instituted; the judge endorses the date of presentation and return, the party presenting and the reasons (Rule 10(2)); Rule 10A — where the court is of opinion after appearance that the plaint should be returned, the plaintiff may apply for an order fixing a date for appearance in the proper court and the summons already served operate in that court; Rule 10B — powers of the appellate court. Return of plaint is not a dismissal; the suit is treated as freshly instituted in the proper court, subject to Sec 14 of the Limitation Act, unless the appellate court directs continuation (Joginder Tuli v. S.L. Bhatia, 1997; Oil and Natural Gas Corporation v. Modern Construction, 2014 — after return, the earlier proceedings do not continue, and the plaintiff must re-present). **Rule 11 — rejection of plaint:** the plaint shall be rejected where (a) it does not disclose a cause of action; (b) the relief claimed is undervalued and the plaintiff fails to correct the valuation within the time fixed; (c) it is insufficiently stamped and the plaintiff fails to supply the requisite stamp paper within the time fixed; (d) the suit appears from the statement in the plaint to be barred by any law; (e) it is not filed in duplicate; (f) the plaintiff fails to comply with Rule 9 — and the time under (b) and (c) shall not be extended except where the court is satisfied that the plaintiff was prevented by a cause of an exceptional nature (1999). Only the plaint is looked at, taken as a whole, not the written statement or evidence (Saleem Bhai v. State of Maharashtra, 2003; Sopan Sukhdeo Sable v. Assistant Charity Commissioner, 2004 — clever drafting creating an illusion of a cause of action must be nipped in the bud; Church of Christ Charitable Trust v. Ponniamman Educational Trust, 2012; Dahiben v. Arvindbhai Kalyanji Bhanusali, 2020 — a suit that is ex facie barred by limitation must be rejected); rejection may be at any stage, even at the instance of the court; a rejection is a deemed decree (Sec 2(2)) and appealable; and Rule 13 provides that rejection does not by itself preclude a fresh plaint on the same cause of action. Rule 12: the order records the reasons. **Rules 14-17 — documents:** the plaintiff must enter in a list and produce with the plaint the documents in his possession or power on which he sues or relies, and where a document is not in his possession state in whose possession it is; a document not so produced shall not, without the court's leave, be received in evidence at the hearing (Rule 14(3), 2002), except documents produced for cross-examination or to refresh memory (Rule 14(4)); Rule 15: statement in case of documents not in the plaintiff's possession; Rule 16: suits on lost negotiable instruments; Rule 17: production of shop-books and copies. Rule 18 (omitted in 2002) once allowed later production with leave — the power now flows from Rule 14(3) and Order XIII."));
c.push(H3("The written statement, set-off and counter-claim — Order VIII"));
c.push(P("**Rule 1:** the defendant shall, within thirty days from the date of service of summons, present a written statement of his defence; the court may extend the time up to ninety days from the date of service for reasons recorded in writing — a limit the Supreme Court held to be **directory** for ordinary suits, so the court may, in exceptional cases with costs, accept a written statement even later (Kailash v. Nanhku, 2005; Salem Advocate Bar Association (II), 2005), but **mandatory** in commercial suits under the 2015 Act, where the outer limit is one hundred and twenty days and the right to file is forfeited thereafter (SCG Contracts India v. K.S. Chamankar Infrastructure, 2019). Rule 1A: duty to produce documents on which the defence or a claim for set-off or counter-claim is based, on the same terms as Order VII Rule 14. Rule 2: the defendant must raise by his pleading **all matters which show the suit not to be maintainable**, or that the transaction is void or voidable, and all grounds of defence which, if not raised, would take the plaintiff by surprise or raise issues of fact not arising out of the plaint — fraud, limitation, release, payment, performance, illegality. Rule 3: denial must be specific, not evasive; Rule 4: evasive denial is insufficient; **Rule 5:** every allegation of fact in the plaint, if not denied specifically or by necessary implication, or stated to be not admitted, shall be taken to be admitted except as against a person under disability — but the court may in its discretion require proof of an admitted fact (Rule 5(1) proviso); where the defendant has not filed a written statement the court may pronounce judgment on the facts in the plaint or require proof (Rule 5(2); Balraj Taneja v. Sunil Madan — the court must still be satisfied). **Rule 6 — set-off:** in a suit for the recovery of money the defendant may claim to set off against the plaintiff's demand an **ascertained sum of money legally recoverable** by him from the plaintiff, not exceeding the pecuniary limits of the court, both parties filling the same character as in the plaintiff's suit; the written statement has the effect of a plaint in a cross-suit; a **legal set-off** must be for an ascertained sum, while an **equitable set-off** (recognised by the courts, not by Rule 6) may be claimed for an unascertained sum arising out of the same transaction (Union of India v. Karam Chand Thapar, 2004; Jitendra Kumar Khan v. Peerless General Finance, 2013). **Rule 6A — counter-claim (1976):** the defendant may set up by way of counter-claim any right or claim in respect of a cause of action accruing to him against the plaintiff either before or after the filing of the suit but before he has delivered his defence or before the time for delivering it has expired, whether in the nature of a claim for damages or not; it must not exceed the pecuniary limits of the court; it has the effect of a cross-suit and the court pronounces a final judgment in the same suit on both the original claim and the counter-claim; the plaintiff files a written statement to it (Rule 6A(3)); the counter-claim is treated as a plaint (Rule 6A(4)); Rule 6B: it must be stated specifically; Rule 6C: the plaintiff may apply to have it excluded and tried separately; Rule 6D: the counter-claim proceeds even if the suit is stayed, discontinued or dismissed; Rule 6E: default of the plaintiff to reply; Rule 6F: a balance may be decreed for the defendant; Rule 6G: the rules relating to written statements apply. Cases: Laxmidas Dayabhai Kabrawala v. Nanabhai Chunilal Kabrawala (1964) — a counter-claim may be treated as a plaint of a cross-suit; Ramesh Chand Ardawatiya v. Anil Panjwani (2003) — a counter-claim may be filed after the written statement but the cause of action must have accrued before the defence was delivered, and it cannot be raised after issues are framed and evidence closed; Ashok Kumar Kalra v. Wing Cdr. Surendra Agnihotri (2020) — the court has discretion to permit a counter-claim after the written statement, but not after issues are framed, and the accrual date is the limit. Rule 7: defence or set-off founded on separate grounds stated separately. Rule 8: new grounds of defence arising after institution. Rule 9: no pleading after the written statement other than by way of defence to a set-off or counter-claim shall be presented except by leave of the court — the plaintiff's replication needs leave. **Rule 10:** where a party fails to present the written statement within the time fixed the court shall pronounce judgment against him or make such order as it thinks fit — read with Rule 1, this is a discretion in ordinary suits (Kailash v. Nanhku), and an ex parte judgment must still be a reasoned one."));
c.push(H3("Framing of issues — Order XIV"));
c.push(P("Issues arise when a material proposition of fact or law is affirmed by one party and denied by the other (Rule 1(1)); material propositions are those which a plaintiff must allege to show a right to sue or a defendant to constitute his defence (Rule 1(2)); each material proposition affirmed and denied forms a distinct issue (Rule 1(3)); issues are of fact or of law (Rule 1(4)); at the first hearing, after reading the plaint and written statement, examining the parties under Order X, and hearing the pleaders, the court ascertains the propositions on which the parties are at variance and frames the issues (Rule 1(5)); no issue is needed where the defendant makes no defence (Rule 1(6)). Rule 2: the court shall pronounce judgment on all issues, but where it is of opinion that the case may be disposed of on an issue of law only, it may try that issue first if it relates to the jurisdiction of the court or a bar to the suit created by any law (Rule 2(2), 1976 — preliminary issues of law only; Ramesh B. Desai v. Bipin Vadilal Mehta, 2006 — limitation is a mixed question and cannot ordinarily be tried as a preliminary issue). Rule 3: materials from which issues may be framed — allegations on oath, pleadings, documents. Rule 4: the court may examine witnesses or documents before framing issues. Rule 5: the court may amend or strike out issues at any time before passing a decree. Rule 6-7: questions of fact or law may be stated in the form of issues by agreement, and the court may pronounce judgment on them. The purpose is to pin down the controversy so that evidence is confined to it and the judgment addresses every disputed point; a judgment without issues is not a nullity if the parties knew the case and led evidence (Nedunuri Kameswaramma v. Sampati Subba Rao, 1963)."));
c.push(CASE("T. Arivandandam v. T.V. Satyapal (1977) 4 SCC 467","A trial court must read the plaint as a whole and, if it discloses no cause of action or is manifestly vexatious, reject it under Order VII Rule 11 at the threshold; the court should not be a party to abuse of process."));
c.push(CASE("Revajeetu Builders and Developers v. Narayanaswamy and Sons (2009) 10 SCC 84","Consolidated principles for amendment under Order VI Rule 17: necessity for the real controversy, no injustice that costs cannot compensate, no change of the nature of the suit, no time-barred claim, no withdrawal of admissions, bona fides, and the due-diligence proviso after trial commences."));
c.push(CASE("Kailash v. Nanhku (2005) 4 SCC 480","The ninety-day limit in Order VIII Rule 1 is directory in ordinary suits; the court may extend it in exceptional cases on costs — but not routinely (the 2015 Act makes it mandatory in commercial suits: SCG Contracts, 2019)."));
c.push(CASE("Saleem Bhai v. State of Maharashtra (2003) 1 SCC 557","For rejection under Order VII Rule 11 only the averments in the plaint are germane; the written statement is irrelevant; the power may be exercised at any stage before the conclusion of the trial."));
c.push(CASE("Ramesh Chand Ardawatiya v. Anil Panjwani (2003) 7 SCC 350","A counter-claim under Order VIII Rule 6A may be filed with or after the written statement but before issues are framed and evidence closed; the cause of action must have accrued before the defence was delivered."));
c.push(DIG("E-filing rules require the plaint, its verification affidavit and the Order VII Rule 14 list of documents to be uploaded as searchable PDFs with digital signatures; the plaint's statement of value is auto-checked against the court-fee calculator. Commercial Courts require a 'statement of truth' (Order VI Rule 15A) in place of ordinary verification and disclose electronic records — e-mails, WhatsApp chats, server logs — in the Order XI list, which then need Sec 63 BSA certificates to be proved. Pleadings in cyber-contract disputes must plead the electronic execution (click-wrap acceptance, IP address, timestamp) as material facts under Order VI Rule 2, not merely attach printouts."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Material facts', 'Amendment of pleadings', 'Rejection of plaint', 'Return of plaint', 'Set-off', 'Counter-claim', 'Issues'. Part B: 'What are the fundamental rules of pleading? When can pleadings be amended?' — Order VI Rule 2, 4, 6-13, then Rule 17 with the proviso and Revajeetu principles; or 'State the grounds on which a plaint may be rejected and the effect of rejection'; or 'Distinguish set-off from counter-claim'. Part C: a defendant files a written statement on the one hundredth day — Kailash v. Nanhku (ordinary suit, discretion) versus SCG Contracts (commercial suit, barred); a plaint on a sale agreement barred on its face by limitation — reject under Rule 11(d) (Dahiben)."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 05 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 05 — The Day of Hearing: Salem Advocate Bar Association and the Trial"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Appearance of parties and ex parte procedure (Order IX) · Examination of parties (Order X) · Admissions (Order XII) · Production of documents (Order XIII) · Summoning and attendance of witnesses (Order XVI) · Hearing and examination of witnesses, evidence on affidavit (Order XVIII) · Affidavits (Order XIX) · Adjournments and arrears (Order XVII)"));
c.push(STORY("Salem, Tamil Nadu, 2002. The 1999 and 2002 amendments to the Code have just come into force with time limits everywhere: thirty days for a written statement, three adjournments to a party, evidence-in-chief on affidavit, no appeal against a Sec 115 revision, no letters patent appeal. The Salem Advocate Bar Association challenges them as unconstitutional, unworkable and destructive of the adversarial trial — how can a witness's evidence-in-chief be an affidavit drafted by his lawyer? The Supreme Court in the first Salem case (2003) upheld the amendments but, unusually, appointed a committee under Justice M. Jagannadha Rao to examine how they would work and to draft model rules. In the second Salem case (2005) the Court accepted the committee's report and explained every contested provision: the ninety-day limit for a written statement is directory, but the court must record reasons for extending it; three adjournments is the rule but a court may grant more in a case of genuine hardship on costs; the affidavit under Order XVIII Rule 4 is the examination-in-chief and the witness must still come for cross-examination; the affidavit under Order VI Rule 15 makes a false pleading a perjury; costs must be real. The judgment became the operating manual for the trial stage of a civil suit — the day of hearing, from the moment the parties are called until the last witness steps down."));
c.push(H3("Appearance and non-appearance — Order IX"));
c.push(P("Rule 1: parties attend on the day fixed in the summons. Rule 2: dismissal where the summons was not served because the plaintiff failed to pay costs or file copies — but not if the defendant appears. Rule 3: where neither party appears the suit is dismissed; Rule 4: the plaintiff may bring a fresh suit or apply to set aside the dismissal. Rule 5: where the summons was not served and the plaintiff fails to apply for fresh summons within seven days (1999), the suit is dismissed against that defendant. Rule 6: where the plaintiff appears and the defendant does not — if the summons was duly served the court may proceed **ex parte**; if not duly served, fresh summons; if served but not in time, the hearing is adjourned. Rule 7: where the court has adjourned the hearing ex parte and the defendant appears at or before the adjourned hearing and shows good cause, he may be heard as if he had appeared — otherwise he may join the proceedings from the stage he appears (Sangram Singh; Arjun Singh v. Mohindra Kumar, 1964 — an order under Rule 7 is not res judicata for a later Rule 13 application). Rule 8: where the defendant appears and the plaintiff does not, the suit is dismissed unless the defendant admits the claim; **Rule 9:** a plaintiff whose suit is so dismissed is precluded from bringing a fresh suit on the same cause of action, but may apply to set aside the dismissal on sufficient cause with notice to the defendant. Rule 10-11: death or non-appearance of one of several plaintiffs or defendants. Rule 12: a party who appears but does not show sufficient cause for a previous non-appearance is treated as not appearing. **Rule 13 — setting aside an ex parte decree:** the defendant may apply to the court that passed it, and the court shall set it aside on satisfaction that the summons was not duly served or that he was prevented by any sufficient cause from appearing, on such terms as to costs, payment into court or otherwise; where the decree is against some defendants only it may be set aside against all if it cannot be severed; the second proviso (1976) — no decree is set aside merely on an irregularity in service if the defendant had notice in time to appear; the Explanation (1976) — where an appeal against the ex parte decree has been disposed of on any ground other than withdrawal, no application under Rule 13 lies. Rule 14: no decree is set aside without notice to the opposite party. 'Sufficient cause' is construed liberally to advance substantial justice (G.P. Srivastava v. R.K. Raizada, 2000 — the test is whether the party was prevented from appearing on the date of the ex parte hearing; his earlier conduct is not decisive; Parimal v. Veena, 2011 — 'sufficient cause' means the party did not act negligently or in a way that shows want of bona fides). The remedies against an ex parte decree are alternatives that may be pursued together: an application under Order IX Rule 13, an appeal under Sec 96(2), a review, and a suit on the ground of fraud (Bhanu Kumar Jain v. Archana Kumar, 2005 — after the Rule 13 application is dismissed the appeal against the decree may still be pursued, but the defendant cannot argue in that appeal that he had sufficient cause; Rabindra Singh v. Financial Commissioner, 2008). Limitation for a Rule 13 application: thirty days from the decree, or from knowledge where the summons was not duly served (Art 123 Limitation Act)."));
c.push(H3("Examination of parties, admissions and documents — Order X, XII, XIII"));
c.push(P("**Order X:** at the first hearing the court ascertains from each party or his pleader whether he admits or denies the allegations of fact in the pleadings of the other (Rule 1); after recording admissions and denials the court **shall direct the parties to opt for one of the modes of settlement outside the court under Sec 89** — arbitration, conciliation, judicial settlement including Lok Adalat, or mediation — and refer the matter accordingly (Rule 1A, 1999; Afcons Infrastructure v. Cherian Varkey Construction, 2010 — the court need not obtain consent for referral to Lok Adalat, mediation or judicial settlement, but must for arbitration and conciliation; a list of suits unsuitable for reference); Rule 1B-1C: appearance before the forum and return to the court if settlement fails; Rule 2: the court may examine any party orally to elucidate matters in controversy, and record the substance; Rule 3: the examination is recorded and signed; Rule 4: a pleader who refuses or is unable to answer may be required to bring the party. **Order XII:** any party may give notice that he admits the truth of the whole or part of the case of the other (Rule 1); either party may call on the other by notice to admit documents within seven days, and the costs of proving a document not admitted without reasonable cause fall on the refusing party (Rule 2, 1999 — documents not denied within the time are deemed admitted, Rule 2A); notice to admit facts (Rule 4); admissions made on the pleadings or otherwise may be acted upon; **Rule 6 — judgment on admissions:** where admissions of fact have been made either in the pleading or otherwise, whether orally or in writing, the court may at any stage, on the application of any party or of its own motion, make such order or give such judgment as it thinks fit, without waiting for the determination of any other question (Uttam Singh Duggal v. United Bank of India, 2000 — the power is discretionary and to be exercised where the admission is clear and unambiguous; Himani Alloys v. Tata Steel, 2011 — the admission must be unconditional, categorical and not open to doubt; Karam Kapahi v. Lal Chand Public Charitable Trust, 2010 — an admission in a reply to a notice or in an earlier proceeding can be 'otherwise'). **Order XIII:** the parties or their pleaders produce, at or before the settlement of issues, all documentary evidence in original not already produced (Rule 1, 1999 — documents not produced are not received without the court's leave, save documents for cross-examination or refreshing memory); Rule 3: rejection of irrelevant or inadmissible documents with reasons; Rule 4: every document admitted in evidence is endorsed with the number and title of the suit, the name of the producer, the date and a statement of its admission, signed by the judge; Rule 6: endorsement on rejected documents; Rule 7: recording of admitted and return of rejected documents; Rule 8: impounding; Rule 9: return of admitted documents after the suit, on substituting a certified copy; Rule 10: the court may send for records of its own or other courts."));
c.push(H3("Witnesses and the hearing — Order XVI, XVIII, XIX"));
c.push(P("**Order XVI:** on or before the date fixed for settlement of issues (fifteen days after issues — Rule 1(1), 1999) the parties file a **list of witnesses** they propose to call and obtain summons; a party may apply for summons for a witness not in the list with reasons (Rule 1(3)); a party may bring any witness without summons (Rule 1A); the applicant deposits the witness's expenses within seven days (Rule 2); the summons states the time, place and purpose — to give evidence or produce a document (Rule 5-6); a witness may be ordered to attend in person (Rule 7); summons is served like a summons to a defendant, or through the party himself (Rule 7A); a witness who fails to attend without lawful excuse may be proceeded against by proclamation, attachment of property and fine (Rule 10-12, and Rule 10 requires the court to be satisfied that the witness is avoiding service); Rule 14: the court may of its own motion summon a stranger to the suit as a witness; Rule 19-21: no witness is ordered to attend in person outside certain distances; Order XVI-A (1976): attendance of prisoners. **Order XVIII — hearing:** the plaintiff has the right to begin unless the defendant admits the facts and contends that on a point of law or on additional facts the plaintiff is not entitled to relief (Rule 1); the party beginning states his case and produces his evidence, then the other party, then the reply (Rule 2); the court may direct that any party's evidence be recorded on a preliminary issue first (Rule 2(4)); a party may adduce evidence on issues on which the burden lies on him and reserve evidence on other issues by way of answer (Rule 3); Rule 3A (1976): a party who wishes to appear as a witness must appear before any other witness on his behalf, unless the court permits otherwise (Maharaja of Kolhapur? — the rule is directory, Janki Vashdeo Bhojwani v. Indusind Bank, 2005 is on a power-of-attorney holder's evidence); **Rule 4 (2002): in every case the examination-in-chief of a witness shall be on affidavit** and copies supplied to the opposite party; the evidence — cross-examination and re-examination — of the witness in attendance is taken either by the court or by a commissioner appointed by it (Rule 4(2)), recorded in the presence of the judge or commissioner, and the court may record remarks on demeanour; the affidavit-evidence rule applies to witnesses who are summoned as well (Ameer Trading Corporation v. Shapoorji Data Processing, 2004 — the affidavit is the chief examination; the witness must be produced for cross-examination; an affidavit without the witness is no evidence; Rasiklal Manikchand Dhariwal v. M.S.S. Food Products, 2012 — the affidavit must be limited to facts within the deponent's knowledge; the court may reject inadmissible matter); Rule 5: in appealable cases the evidence is taken down in the language of the court by or in the presence of the judge, or by mechanical means (Rule 5(b), 1999) or from dictation; Rule 6-8: evidence in a language the witness does not understand, and memorandum of evidence in non-appealable cases; Rule 9-13: remarks, adjournment of the case for want of witnesses, the judge's note of objections (Rule 11), remarks on demeanour (Rule 12), memorandum when the evidence is not taken down by the judge; Rule 15: power to deal with evidence taken before another judge; Rule 16: the court may take the evidence of a witness about to leave the jurisdiction immediately; Rule 17: the court may recall and examine a witness (the court's power, not the party's — Vadiraj Naggappa Vernekar v. Sharadchandra Prabhakar Gogate, 2009); Rule 17A (deleted in 1999) once allowed production of evidence not previously known; Rule 18: the court may inspect property or thing; Rule 19 (1999): the court may direct that the evidence of a witness be recorded by a commissioner. **Order XIX — affidavits:** the court may at any time order that any particular fact be proved by affidavit, or that the affidavit of a witness be read at the hearing, on such conditions as it thinks reasonable — but not where the other party bona fide desires the production of the witness for cross-examination and he can be produced (Rule 1); evidence on affidavit may be ordered on applications, and the deponent may be cross-examined (Rule 2); affidavits are confined to such facts as the deponent is able of his own knowledge to prove, except on interlocutory applications, on which statements of belief may be admitted with the grounds (Rule 3); Sec 139: affidavits may be sworn before a court, magistrate, notary or an officer appointed by the High Court. The 2002 amendments to Rule 4 and the new Rule 15(4) of Order VI (verification on affidavit) together mean that the bulk of a civil trial now takes place on paper, with oral evidence limited to cross-examination."));
c.push(H3("Adjournments and arrears — Order XVII"));
c.push(P("**Rule 1 (as amended in 1999):** the court may, if sufficient cause is shown, at any stage of the suit grant time to the parties and adjourn the hearing, for reasons to be recorded in writing — **provided that no such adjournment shall be granted more than three times to a party during the hearing of the suit**; Rule 1(2): the court fixes a day for the further hearing and may make such orders as to costs occasioned by the adjournment or higher costs as it thinks fit; provisos (1999): when the hearing has commenced it shall continue from day to day until all the witnesses in attendance have been examined unless the court finds adjournment beyond the following day necessary for reasons to be recorded; no adjournment is granted at the request of a party except where the circumstances are beyond his control; the pleader's engagement in another court is not a ground; the illness of the pleader or his inability to conduct the case for a reason other than engagement elsewhere is a ground only if the court is satisfied that the party could not have engaged another pleader in time; where a witness is present and a party or his pleader is not present or is unable to examine or cross-examine the witness, the court may record the statement of the witness and pass such orders as it thinks fit dispensing with the examination or cross-examination. Salem Advocate Bar Association (II) read the three-adjournment cap as a strong rule but held that in a case of genuine hardship — illness, accident, a matter beyond control — the court retains the power to adjourn beyond three on heavy costs, because a rule that shuts out a party altogether would defeat the purpose of the Code; the proviso about the pleader's engagement is mandatory. **Rule 2:** where on an adjourned hearing the parties or any of them fail to appear, the court may proceed to dispose of the suit in one of the modes directed by Order IX or make such other order as it thinks fit — and the Explanation (1976) provides that where the evidence or a substantial portion of the evidence of a party has already been recorded and he fails to appear, the court may proceed as if he were present. **Rule 3:** where a party to whom time was granted fails to produce evidence, cause the attendance of witnesses, or perform any other act necessary for the further progress of the suit, the court may, notwithstanding the default, if the parties are present, proceed to decide the suit forthwith, or, if any party is absent, proceed under Rule 2 (Prakash Chander Manchanda v. Janki Manchanda, 1986 — where the defaulting party is absent the court should proceed under Order IX so that the remedy of setting aside remains). The problem of **arrears**: the Law Commission's 14th, 54th, 77th and 79th Reports, the Malimath Committee on Arrears (1990) and the 129th Report on urban litigation identified adjournments as the principal cause; the 1999-2002 amendments, the Commercial Courts Act's Order XV-A case management hearings, and the e-Courts case-flow rules are the responses. Judicial discretion remains: the Supreme Court in Shiv Cotex v. Tirgun Auto Plast (2011) said that a court that grants adjournment after adjournment fails in its duty and that a party who does not lead evidence after three opportunities must face the consequences."));
c.push(CASE("Salem Advocate Bar Association v. Union of India (I) (2003) 1 SCC 49 and (II) (2005) 6 SCC 344","The 1999 and 2002 amendments are valid; a committee's model rules were accepted; Order VIII Rule 1 is directory; Order XVII Rule 1's three-adjournment limit yields to genuine hardship on costs; Order XVIII Rule 4 affidavits are the chief examination; Sec 89 requires the court to formulate the terms of settlement only in outline; costs must be actual and realistic."));
c.push(CASE("G.P. Srivastava v. R.K. Raizada (2000) 3 SCC 54","Under Order IX Rule 13 the question is whether the defendant was prevented by sufficient cause from appearing on the date of the ex parte hearing; his earlier negligence is not decisive; a liberal approach advances substantial justice."));
c.push(CASE("Bhanu Kumar Jain v. Archana Kumar (2005) 1 SCC 787","An appeal against an ex parte decree under Sec 96(2) and an application under Order IX Rule 13 are concurrent remedies; after dismissal of the Rule 13 application the appellant cannot re-agitate sufficient cause in the appeal but may challenge the decree on merits."));
c.push(CASE("Ameer Trading Corporation v. Shapoorji Data Processing (2004) 1 SCC 702","Order XVIII Rule 4 affidavits stand as examination-in-chief for all witnesses, including those summoned; the witness must be produced for cross-examination; the Evidence Act's rules on the contents of chief examination apply to the affidavit."));
c.push(CASE("Afcons Infrastructure Ltd. v. Cherian Varkey Construction Co. (2010) 8 SCC 24","Under Sec 89 and Order X Rule 1A the court must consider reference to one of five modes; arbitration and conciliation require consent, the others do not; a list of cases suitable and unsuitable for ADR; the court need not draft detailed settlement terms."));
c.push(DIG("Evidence of witnesses is recorded by video conferencing under the High Courts' 2020 rules and Order XVIII Rule 4 affidavits are e-filed with digital signatures; a witness abroad is examined through the Indian embassy or a commissioner on video. Order XII notices to admit documents are served electronically and deemed admissions are computed by the e-filing system's timestamps. Adjournment data from the National Judicial Data Grid now shows for every court how many adjournments each case has had, which is how High Courts monitor compliance with Order XVII Rule 1. Electronic records produced under Order XIII — CCTV, chat exports, server logs — must be listed and their Sec 63 BSA certificates filed with them."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Ex parte decree and remedies', 'Sufficient cause', 'Judgment on admissions', 'Evidence on affidavit', 'Adjournment', 'Sec 89'. Part B: 'Discuss the consequences of non-appearance of parties under Order IX and the remedies available to a defendant against an ex parte decree' — Rules 6, 7, 8, 9, 13 with the provisos, the four concurrent remedies, Bhanu Kumar Jain, G.P. Srivastava; or 'Examine the changes made by the 1999 and 2002 amendments in the conduct of trial and their validity (Salem Advocate)'. Part C: a defendant seeks a fourth adjournment because his lawyer is in another court — refused under the proviso; a defendant against whom an ex parte decree was passed on the ground of substituted service he never saw — Rule 13, 'not duly served', limitation from knowledge."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 06 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 06 — Prima Facie, Balance, Irreparable: Dalpat Kumar and the Court's Interim Hand"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Judgment and decree (Sec 33, Order XX) · Preliminary and final decrees · Interim orders and stay · Temporary injunctions (Sec 94, Order XXXIX) · Appointment of receivers (Order XL) · Arrest and attachment before judgment (Order XXXVIII) · Costs (Sec 35-35B) · Inherent powers (Sec 151) · Restitution (Sec 144)"));
c.push(STORY("Jaipur, 1991. Dalpat Kumar bought a house at a court auction in execution of a decree against Prahlad Singh's family. Years after the sale was confirmed, Prahlad Singh filed a suit claiming that the sale was fraudulent and obtained a temporary injunction restraining the purchaser from taking possession. The Supreme Court set the injunction aside and, in doing so, wrote the three-part test that is applied in every interim application in every civil court in the country: the applicant must show a **prima facie case** — a serious question to be tried, not a case certain to succeed; that the **balance of convenience** favours him — the comparative mischief of granting or refusing the injunction; and that he would suffer **irreparable injury** that money cannot repair. All three must exist, and the court must record its satisfaction on each. An injunction, the Court reminded, is an equitable relief and the plaintiff who has delayed, who has come with unclean hands, or whose own conduct is inequitable cannot have it. Between the filing of a suit and its decree years may pass, and this episode is about what the court can do in the meantime — stop a party from acting, take property into its own hands through a receiver, secure the decree by attachment before judgment — and about the decree itself: how the judgment is written, what a preliminary decree leaves for later, and how costs and restitution close the account."));
c.push(H3("Judgment and decree — Sec 33 and Order XX"));
c.push(P("**Sec 33:** the court, after the case has been heard, shall pronounce judgment, and on such judgment a decree shall follow. **Order XX Rule 1 (1999):** judgment is pronounced in open court at once or, where practicable, within thirty days from the conclusion of the hearing, and in exceptional circumstances within sixty days, after notice to the parties; a judgment once written and signed may be read out; Rule 2: a judge may pronounce a judgment written but not pronounced by his predecessor; Rule 3: the judgment is dated and signed in open court and cannot be altered or added to afterwards except under Sec 152 (clerical errors) or on review; **Rule 4:** judgments of a Small Cause Court contain the points for determination and the decision; other judgments contain **a concise statement of the case, the points for determination, the decision thereon and the reasons** (the four requirements — a judgment that does not decide every issue with reasons is defective; Balraj Taneja v. Sunil Madan, 1999); Rule 5: the court states its finding on each separate issue; Rule 5A: the court informs the parties about the appeal and the period of limitation; Rule 6: the decree agrees with the judgment, contains the number of the suit, the names and descriptions of the parties, the particulars of the claim, the relief granted and the costs, and states by whom and out of what property costs are paid; Rule 6A (1976): the decree is drawn up within fifteen days of the judgment, and an appeal may be preferred on the judgment if the decree is not drawn up; Rule 6B (1999): copies of the judgment are made available immediately on payment; Rule 7: the decree bears the date of the judgment and is signed by the judge; Rule 8: procedure where the judge has vacated office; Rule 9-11: decrees for recovery of immovable property, for delivery of movable property, and for payment of money by instalments; Rule 12: decree for possession and mesne profits — the decree may direct an inquiry into mesne profits and a **final decree** follows; Rule 12A: decree for specific performance directs the time for payment; Rule 13: decree in administration suits; Rule 14: decree in pre-emption suits; Rule 15: decree in suit for dissolution of partnership — preliminary decree declaring shares, then accounts; Rule 16: decree in suit for account between principal and agent; Rule 17: special directions as to accounts; **Rule 18: decree in suit for partition** — where the property is an estate assessed to revenue the decree declares the rights and the partition is made by the Collector (Sec 54); in other cases the court passes a preliminary decree declaring the rights and may direct a commission for the actual division, followed by a final decree; Rule 19: decree when set-off or counter-claim is allowed; Rule 20: certified copies. A **preliminary decree** determines the rights of the parties but leaves further proceedings before the suit can be completely disposed of; a **final decree** completely disposes of the suit; there may be more than one preliminary decree, e.g. in a partition suit where shares change on the death of a party (Phoolchand v. Gopal Lal, 1967), and the final decree in a partition suit may be passed on the court's own initiative without a separate application (S. Sai Reddy v. S. Narayana Reddy, 1991 — a preliminary decree in partition can be amended to give effect to a change in law, such as a daughter's share under the 2005 amendment; Venkata Reddy v. Pethi Reddy, 1963 — a preliminary decree is final as to what it decides and is appealable, and if not appealed cannot be questioned in the appeal from the final decree — Sec 97)."));
c.push(H3("Temporary injunctions — Sec 94(c) and Order XXXIX"));
c.push(P("**Rule 1:** where in any suit it is proved by affidavit or otherwise that (a) any property in dispute is in danger of being wasted, damaged or alienated by any party, or wrongfully sold in execution of a decree, or (b) the defendant threatens or intends to remove or dispose of his property with a view to defrauding his creditors, or (c) the defendant threatens to dispossess the plaintiff or otherwise cause injury to the plaintiff in relation to any property in dispute — the court may grant a temporary injunction to restrain such act, or make such other order for staying and preventing the wasting, damaging, alienation, sale, removal or disposition of the property or dispossession or injury as the court thinks fit, until disposal of the suit or further orders. **Rule 2:** injunction to restrain the repetition or continuance of a breach of contract or other injury of any kind, whether or not compensation is claimed; **Rule 2A (1976):** consequences of disobedience or breach — attachment of property and detention in civil prison for up to three months, the attachment not to remain beyond one year, after which the property may be sold and compensation awarded to the injured party (Samee Khan v. Bindu Khan, 1998 — the two remedies are alternative or cumulative, and the court may punish disobedience of an injunction granted under Rule 1 or 2 or under Sec 151; Food Corporation of India v. Sukh Deo Prasad, 2009 — Rule 2A is available for disobedience of an injunction, not for enforcing an undertaking or a consent decree). **Rule 3:** the court shall in all cases, except where it appears that the object of granting the injunction would be defeated by the delay, before granting an injunction direct notice to the opposite party — and the 1976 proviso: where an **ex parte** injunction is granted the court shall record the reasons for its opinion that delay would defeat the object, and require the applicant to deliver to the opposite party a copy of the application, affidavit, plaint and documents immediately after the order and to file an affidavit of compliance on the same or the next day (Shiv Kumar Chadha v. Municipal Corporation of Delhi, 1993 — recording of reasons is mandatory; an ex parte injunction without reasons is liable to be set aside; the remedy against an ex parte order is an application under Rule 4, and an appeal under Order XLIII Rule 1(r) also lies); **Rule 3A (1976):** where an injunction is granted without notice the court shall make an endeavour to dispose of the application within thirty days, and where it is unable to do so it shall record its reasons (A. Venkatasubbiah Naidu v. S. Chellappan, 2000 — the thirty-day rule is mandatory in the sense that the party may appeal if the court fails to decide, but the order does not lapse automatically); **Rule 4:** an order for injunction may be discharged, varied or set aside on application by any party dissatisfied, and where it was obtained by false or misleading statements it shall be vacated unless the court for reasons to be recorded considers this unnecessary; the court will not vary an order on the same material without a change of circumstances (Rule 4 provisos, 1976); Rule 5: an injunction directed to a corporation binds its officers. The three principles — **prima facie case, balance of convenience, irreparable injury** — are settled by Dalpat Kumar v. Prahlad Singh (1992), Gujarat Bottling Co. v. Coca Cola Co. (1995 — the court must also consider the conduct of the applicant, delay and whether he approached with clean hands; injunction is an equitable remedy), Morgan Stanley Mutual Fund v. Kartick Das (1994 — ex parte injunctions should be granted only where the injury is imminent and irreparable, the balance of convenience is overwhelming, and the applicant acted promptly; a court should consider whether the applicant will be able to compensate the defendant), Colgate Palmolive v. Hindustan Lever (1999 — the court must weigh 'the comparative mischief'), Seema Arshad Zaheer v. Municipal Corporation of Greater Mumbai (2006), and Best Sellers Retail v. Aditya Birla Nuvo (2012 — even when a prima facie case is made out the injunction may be refused if the plaintiff can be compensated in money). Mandatory injunctions at the interim stage are granted only in exceptional cases to restore the status quo ante (Dorab Cawasji Warden v. Coomi Sorab Warden, 1990). The court has an inherent power to grant an injunction in cases not covered by Order XXXIX (Manohar Lal Chopra v. Seth Hiralal, 1962 — Sec 151 may be used to restrain a party from proceeding with a suit in another court). An interim injunction ends with the suit; a perpetual injunction is granted by the decree under Sec 37-42 of the Specific Relief Act. Stay of a decree under appeal is under Order XLI Rule 5, not Order XXXIX."));
c.push(H3("Receivers — Order XL"));
c.push(P("Rule 1: where it appears to the court to be **just and convenient**, it may by order appoint a receiver of any property, whether before or after decree; remove any person from possession or custody; commit it to the receiver; and confer on him all such powers as to bringing and defending suits, and for the realisation, management, protection, preservation and improvement of the property, the collection of rents and profits, and the application and disposal of them, and the execution of documents, as the owner himself has — but the court shall not remove from possession a person whom a party to the suit has no present right to remove. Rule 2: remuneration; Rule 3: the receiver furnishes security, submits accounts, pays the amount due, and is responsible for loss caused by wilful default or gross negligence; Rule 4: enforcement of the receiver's duties by attachment and sale of his property; Rule 5: a Collector may be appointed receiver of land paying revenue. The five principles of **T. Krishnaswamy Chetty v. C. Thangavelu Chetty (1955, Madras)**, adopted by the Supreme Court: appointment of a receiver is a harsh remedy to be granted with great care; the court will not appoint a receiver merely because it will do no harm; the applicant must show a good prima facie case — an excellent chance of success; the object is to preserve the property pending the suit; the court will look at the conduct of the applicant. A receiver is an officer of the court, neutral between the parties, and his possession is the court's possession; interference with him is contempt. The receiver is not appointed to dispossess a party in lawful possession unless there is danger to the property (Parmanand Patel v. Sudha A. Chowgule, 2009)."));
c.push(H3("Arrest and attachment before judgment — Order XXXVIII"));
c.push(P("Rules 1-4: where the court is satisfied by affidavit or otherwise that the defendant, with intent to delay the plaintiff or to avoid any process of the court or to obstruct or delay the execution of any decree that may be passed, is about to abscond or leave the local limits, or has disposed of or removed his property or is about to do so, or that the defendant is about to leave India in circumstances affording reasonable probability that the plaintiff will be obstructed or delayed in the execution of a decree, the court may issue a warrant to arrest the defendant and bring him before the court to show cause why he should not furnish security for his appearance — but he may avoid arrest by paying the amount into court; on failure to furnish security he may be committed to civil prison for up to six weeks (or six months if the claim exceeds fifty rupees). **Rule 5:** where the court is satisfied that the defendant, with intent to obstruct or delay the execution of any decree, is about to dispose of the whole or part of his property, or to remove it from the local limits, the court may direct him to furnish security in a specified sum and to produce and place at the disposal of the court the property or its value, or to show cause why he should not furnish security; the court may order **conditional attachment** of the property specified; an attachment made without complying with Rule 5(1) is void (Rule 5(4), 1976). Rule 6: where the defendant fails to show cause or to furnish security the court may attach the property. Rule 7-8: the mode of attachment is as in execution, and claims to attached property are adjudicated as in execution (Order XXI Rule 58). Rule 9: withdrawal of attachment when security is furnished or the suit is dismissed. Rule 10: attachment before judgment does not affect the rights of persons not parties existing before the attachment, and the decree-holder need not apply for attachment in execution (Rule 11). Rule 11A (1976): the provisions of the Code applicable to attachment in execution apply. Rule 12: agricultural produce is not attached before judgment; Rule 13: Small Cause Courts cannot attach immovable property. The power is drastic and requires a specific satisfaction on the intent to defeat the decree — a bare allegation is not enough (Raman Tech and Process Engineering Co. v. Solanki Traders, 2008 — the purpose is not to convert an unsecured debt into a secured one; Premraj Mundra v. Md. Maneck Gazi, 1951 — Calcutta guidelines)."));
c.push(H3("Costs, inherent powers and restitution"));
c.push(P("**Sec 35:** costs of and incident to all suits are in the discretion of the court, which has full power to determine by whom and out of what property and to what extent costs are paid; where costs do not follow the event the court states its reasons; the 2015 Act substitutes Sec 35 for commercial disputes with the general rule that costs follow the event and include lawyers' fees; Salem Advocate (II) directed that costs be actual and realistic. **Sec 35A (1922):** compensatory costs up to three thousand rupees in respect of false or vexatious claims or defences, on the objection of the other party; **Sec 35B (1976):** costs for causing delay — where a party fails to take a step or obtains an adjournment, the court may order him to pay the costs of the other party on that date, and payment is a condition precedent to further prosecution of the suit or defence (Ashok Kumar v. Ram Kumar, 2010 — the consequence of non-payment is that the defaulting party is not permitted to proceed further, not dismissal of the suit); Order XX-A: costs of notices, typing, witness expenses. **Sec 151:** nothing in the Code limits or affects the inherent power of the court to make such orders as may be necessary for the ends of justice or to prevent abuse of the process of the court — used to stay suits not covered by Sec 10, to grant injunctions outside Order XXXIX, to recall orders obtained by fraud, to consolidate suits, to set aside ex parte orders on applications, to expunge remarks — but not where the Code expressly provides for the matter, not to override an express provision, and not to create substantive rights (Padam Sen v. State of U.P., 1961 — no inherent power to appoint a commissioner to seize account books; Ram Chand and Sons Sugar Mills v. Kanhayalal Bhargava, 1966; Manohar Lal Chopra, 1962; Durgesh Sharma v. Jayshree, 2008 — Sec 151 cannot be used to transfer a suit from one State to another when Sec 25 provides for it; Vinod Seth v. Devinder Bajaj, 2010 — Sec 151 cannot be used to direct a plaintiff to furnish security in a suit for specific performance). **Sec 152-153A:** clerical or arithmetical errors in judgments, decrees or orders may be corrected at any time; general power to amend proceedings; an amendment of the decree on a compromise. **Sec 144 — restitution:** where a decree or order is varied or reversed in appeal, revision or other proceeding, or is set aside or modified in a suit instituted for the purpose, the court which passed the decree may, on the application of a party entitled to a benefit by way of restitution, cause such restitution to be made as will place the parties in the position they would have occupied but for the decree, and for this purpose may make orders for the refund of costs, payment of interest, damages, compensation and mesne profits — the principle that no one should suffer by the act of the court (actus curiae neminem gravabit — Binayak Swain v. Ramesh Chandra Panigrahi, 1966; South Eastern Coalfields v. State of M.P., 2003 — restitution includes compensation for the benefit obtained under the erroneous order, and a party who took the benefit of an interim order cannot say he was not unjustly enriched); the application is made to the court of first instance, and no separate suit lies (Sec 144(2)); a determination under Sec 144 is a decree."));
c.push(CASE("Dalpat Kumar v. Prahlad Singh (1992) 1 SCC 719","The three conditions for a temporary injunction — prima facie case, balance of convenience, irreparable injury — are cumulative and the court must record its satisfaction on each; a purchaser at a court sale confirmed years earlier cannot be restrained from taking possession on a belated allegation of fraud."));
c.push(CASE("Gujarat Bottling Co. Ltd. v. Coca Cola Co. (1995) 5 SCC 545","Interim injunction is an equitable relief; in addition to the three tests the court considers the conduct of the party seeking it, delay, and whether he has acted fairly; the court's discretion under Order XXXIX is judicial."));
c.push(CASE("Morgan Stanley Mutual Fund v. Kartick Das (1994) 4 SCC 225","Ex parte injunctions are exceptional; factors: imminent irreparable mischief, overwhelming balance of convenience, prompt application, the applicant's ability to compensate, and that the injunction is for the shortest period until the other side is heard."));
c.push(CASE("Shiv Kumar Chadha v. Municipal Corporation of Delhi (1993) 3 SCC 161","Recording reasons under the proviso to Order XXXIX Rule 3 is mandatory for an ex parte injunction; the party aggrieved may apply under Rule 4 or appeal under Order XLIII Rule 1(r)."));
c.push(CASE("South Eastern Coalfields Ltd. v. State of M.P. (2003) 8 SCC 648","Restitution under Sec 144 and the inherent power restore the party to the position before the erroneous order; interest and compensation may be awarded so that no one gains an advantage from a court's order that is later reversed."));
c.push(DIG("Order XXXIX injunctions now routinely restrain online conduct — takedown of defamatory posts, blocking of infringing websites (dynamic 'John Doe' injunctions, UTV Software v. 1337x.to, Delhi HC 2019), and freezing of cryptocurrency wallets by analogy with attachment before judgment. Receivers are appointed over websites and social media accounts, and the receiver's reports are filed electronically. Costs under the Commercial Courts Act are computed from e-filed bills of costs, and the NJDG flags cases pending interim relief beyond the thirty-day limit of Rule 3A."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Preliminary decree', 'Temporary injunction', 'Receiver', 'Attachment before judgment', 'Restitution', 'Inherent powers', 'Compensatory costs'. Part B: 'State the principles on which a court grants a temporary injunction; what is the procedure and the consequence of disobedience?' — Rule 1-2 grounds, the three tests with Dalpat Kumar, Gujarat Bottling, Morgan Stanley, Rule 3 proviso and Shiv Kumar Chadha, Rule 3A, Rule 4, Rule 2A; or 'Discuss the court's power to appoint a receiver and the principles governing it'. Part C: a plaintiff who has waited five years applies for an injunction to stop construction that is nearly complete — delay and balance of convenience defeat it; an ex parte injunction granted without reasons — set aside under Rule 4."));
c.push(LN());

module.exports=c;
